okay I'm considering leaving social  media forever period like just getting  off of social media altogether which is  sort of a difficult thing to do if you  host a show like this one that relies so  much on social media and on being in  touch with people on social media and  using social media to spread the content  that we make and I know that there are  some people in the audience who will  sort of react to this by saying why on  earth would you leave social media but  based on some of the emails I receive  and that's a key word emails I know that  there are lots of people in the audience  who will immediately understand why I'm  thinking of leaving social media  altogether and I could go into sort of  like a long sophisticated explanation of  why but I I don't think I really need to  social media is almost exclusively toxic  as a venue in which people exist and  interact and look at stuff and I'm gonna  kind of build the case up for you one  step at a time  first is the kind of like general  perspective later I'll talk about how it  applies to me as the host of a political  show but Jonathan height for example has  written extensively about the extremely  negative effect of just normal social  media usage on preteens and on high  schoolers at a psychological level and  even for adults the data is more and  more prevalent and overwhelming as far  as this goes which is that spending time  on social media scrolling through your  Facebook feed Twitter feed  it's just bad for our health it's bad  for our psychological health and our  physical health and there's on the one  hand the kind of addictive nature which  is similar to a slot machine just like  you can go back to the slot machine and  pull the lever one more time and maybe  you get something good you can always  scroll down a little bit more down your  Twitter or Facebook feed and you might  get something good and this is this goes  back to operant conditioning this is a  variable reward schedule that applies to  slot machines and it applies to social  media it sort of feels like high school  psychology class this is one of the  things that make social media addictive  and never satisfying because you can  always scroll a little more and maybe  see something even  better or more interesting than the last  thing so that's sort of layer one beyond  that we know that when people scroll  social media you're immediately sort of  automatically starting to compare  yourself with what you see comparing  your life with what's there and when you  compare your life in total with the  curated carefully selected moments and  stories and pictures that other people  are choosing to share it's an impossible  standard and even if we understand this  sort of intellectually as I think most  of us do on a subconscious level it  makes social media really bad for us and  it's not that I spend much time on  social media beyond what's necessary for  the show but there's also the kind of  time element of it if you add up all the  little bits of time that are spent on  Twitter and Facebook and Instagram  looking at a comment here checking out a  comment there it starts to add up and it  gets to what I consider to be sort of  the second layer of the problem if you  haven't read - of Cal Newports books  about this you should immediately read  them Cal Newport wrote the book deep  work years ago and it's about the lack  of what he calls deep work in modern  society people's jobs are increasingly  answering emails administrative tasks  all of what I would call shallow work  that's not actually central to what we  do that is valuable in my case like the  real value the deep work that I'm trying  to do is research and prepare stories  for the show to make the content of the  show as good as possible and the best  way to do this is total concentration no  email checking no social media I want to  be doing more of that more of that deep  work so it seems kind of natural to  eliminate as many of those shallow  behaviors from my day that aren't  totally necessary and social media is  one of them and then Cal Newport also  wrote another book more recently called  digital minimalism which I'll interview  him about in a couple of weeks which  goes beyond just the crisis of the lack  of deep work but it's more about the  damaging effect of most people's social  media use and the payoff is pretty  damn clear to me if I spend an hour  deeply learning about economic theory  that will actually pay dividends for the  show by virtue of me being more informed  for years to come  whereas if I spend that hour scrolling  Twitter and Facebook I might find  another topic or two to discuss on the  show maybe but it's not gonna provide  anything that really improves the show  over the long term so without even  getting specifically to what's going on  with my social media yet which I will  the need for doing deep work the waste  of time that most social media use  represents there's a really solid case  for at least drastically minimizing  social media use to specific moments in  the day which has been my strategy so  far okay so the way I have been sort of  managing this is that I have in my  schedule  as far as work goes in the morning  sometime after I finish up preparing the  day's show I'll check all social  networks I'll respond to what I want to  respond to  I'll make notes about things that I want  to follow up on if there are guest  suggestions people sent me your story  suggestions  alright back to personal messages and  then that's it and then late in the  afternoon I'll do another check but the  truth is I have the social apps on my  phone and it's very easy if I'm on the  subway or whatever to just check in on  stuff more often than I'm kind of  consciously trying to so that is all for  everybody that has nothing to do with my  sort of particular situation but then we  get to my social media feed and it's  toxic I mean that's it as many of you  know we get two to three thousand  YouTube comments per day at this point  and I've made it clear that I look at  barely any I mean it's a total cesspool  there's very little benefit other than  I'll look at what videos did well which  videos were heavily liked which videos  were heavily disliked but that's  basically it and unfortunately my  Twitter feed my Facebook feed it is  gotten absolutely horrible and this is  kind of like a curse of success in a  sense the show is now big enough that  any time I go to Twitter there are  dozens if not  of messages just insults attacks  insulting the show some of its from the  right what really sort of is pushing me  towards maybe abandoning social media  all together is the infighting from the  left people on the left saying this  person is not actually left enough or  that person is too far left or this  person is parroting right wing talking  points and obviously in small doses all  of this stuff is fine but the  signal-to-noise ratio has gotten way way  too low in most of these cases and I had  a conversation with with Sam cedar about  this in Vegas a couple of weeks ago and  I said I'm thinking about it minimum  getting off Twitter if not all social  media and he said just mute people so  that you don't see them which is fine  but I have thousands of people muted on  Twitter and I don't really know that I  need to be on these platforms it seems  that there's a lot to gain from  screening them out as an example like do  I need to know that some guy with a  YouTube channel did a 15 minute video  attacking me or will I be basically fine  and the show will be just as good if I  focus on my work rather than looking at  who's doing Twitter posts about me and  the only hesitation I have is that part  of the success of the show is thanks to  social media right social media has been  a very important tool for spreading  awareness and the content of the David  Pakman show so it's weird for me to say  I'm completely getting off of it so I  need to think about it a little bit and  in mid-may I'm going to be interviewing  Cal Newport and this is one of the  things I want to talk to him about which  is does he think I'd be able to get off  of social media given that this is  although it's a radio and television  show it's also significantly an online  show or do I kind of need to grin and  bear it in some sense because of the  importance of social media to the show  maybe I could start looking at just like  verified people's posts on Twitter but  that's problematic because most of our  audience isn't verified on Twitter so  maybe the answer is I just cut it and I  say you know what if you want to get in  touch with me email me actually take the  time to send  me an email I don't know what the answer  is but I'm curious what people think  if you've adopted a sort of digital  minimalism concept into your life I want  to hear about it and one of the really  important things that I want to not  ignore is that whenever people read  about digital minimalism and these types  of issues when people like Cal Newport  write about them the reaction is often a  reflexive that might work for other  people but it wouldn't work for me I  need to be on these platforms because if  X Y Z reason and what I am open to is  the idea that that may just not be the  case like maybe it's not actually that  central to what we're doing so let me  know what you think I want to hear about  your social media use tell me if I'm  completely off on this let me know via  email I would say actually take the time  to write to me and I will eventually I  guess make some kind of decision and let  you know where I land today's show was  made possible in part by better help.com  slash Pakman better help is a service  that gives you unlimited remote access  to your own fully licensed therapist by  way of phone video and text it's less  expensive than physically going to see a  therapist in an office and you don't  need to have insurance to use better  help and they stand behind what they do  better help has a no-questions-asked  full money-back guarantee if you're not  satisfied with your experience all of  better helps counselors are thoroughly  vetted for accreditation and experience  it's all spelled out in their Terms of  Service  better help requires all of its  counselors to be fully trained and  licensed and they must have at least 3  years and 2,000 hours of counseling  experience so you know that your  counselor is qualified to help you but  if you don't think your counselor is the  right fit for you better help makes it  easy to switch to a new one if you've  been thinking about getting involved  with a therapist you should really check  out better help just click the link  underneath this video
 

